        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does kitty litter really work on driveway oil?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for fresh spills. Plain clay litter absorbs free oil well, especially if you grind it into the surface with your shoe so the granules break down and reach into the texture. It works on both asphalt and concrete. It does little for old, set-in stains, because by then the oil has moved below the surface where the litter cannot reach it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is dish soap safe on an asphalt driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Grease-cutting dish soap is one of the safest cleaners for blacktop because it is water-based and does not attack the asphalt binder. Use a generous amount, scrub with a nylon brush, let it sit for about 15 minutes and rinse well. It handles fresh and light stains; heavier ones usually need a water-based degreaser or an enzyme cleaner."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use the same degreaser on asphalt and concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the label says it is safe for asphalt. Many alkaline, water-based degreasers are, but solvent-based and citrus solvent products are not. When in doubt, test a small spot near the edge of the driveway and check after it dries for softening, tackiness or loose stone before treating the main stain."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does it take to get oil off a driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A fresh spill on either surface usually takes an evening: absorbent overnight, then 30 minutes of cleaning the next day. A stain that has sat for weeks on concrete may need two or three rounds over several days. Enzyme cleaners on asphalt can take a week to finish working, with little effort from you in the meantime."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will oil stains damage my asphalt permanently?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can. Oil softens the asphalt binder, and a stain left for weeks may leave a spot that crumbles or stays tacky. Small, cleaned-up spots usually recover once the surface is primed and sealed. Larger soft areas should be cut out and patched with cold patch asphalt before you seal the driveway."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to rinse oil cleaner into the street?",
