    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most oil stain advice is written for concrete and then applied to every driveway, which is how people end up with a soft, pitted dip in their blacktop where the drip used to be. Asphalt is held together by a petroleum binder, so anything strong enough to dissolve motor oil can also dissolve the pavement. Concrete is the opposite problem: it shrugs off solvents but soaks oil deep into its pores. This guide splits the job by surface, tells you what works on each, and shows you when a stain is finished and when it needs another round.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start by Confirming What Your Driveway Is Made Of
          </h2>

          <p>
            It sounds obvious, but plenty of driveways are mixed. A concrete apron at the street with an asphalt run to the garage is common in older subdivisions, and many homes have a concrete garage slab meeting a blacktop driveway at the door. Oil tends to collect exactly at those transitions because that is where cars stop. Before you buy anything, look at the stain and identify which material sits under it, because the two surfaces react to cleaners in opposite ways.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt is black or dark gray, slightly flexible on a hot day, and shows exposed stone once it has weathered for a few years. Concrete is light gray, rigid, and either broom-textured or smooth troweled. Stamped or colored concrete still behaves like concrete. Brick and concrete pavers are their own category, because the joints between them hold sand that can wick oil and that gets washed out if you rinse too hard. If you are not sure, drip a little water on the surface: fresh concrete darkens as it absorbs water, while sealed asphalt beads it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason this matters comes down to chemistry. Asphalt pavement is stone held together with bitumen, a heavy petroleum product. Motor oil, gasoline and solvent-based degreasers are all chemical cousins of that binder, so they soften it. A spill that sits for a week on blacktop does more than stain it; it can leave a spot that stays tacky and crumbles under a tire. Concrete is a mineral surface with tiny capillary pores, so oil does not damage it structurally in normal amounts, but it travels downward and outward, and that is what makes old concrete stains so stubborn.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main risk from oil</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Safe cleaner types</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binder softens, surface ravels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Absorbents, dish soap, water-based degreasers, enzyme cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent degreasers, gasoline, acetone, hard wire brushing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bare concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep, spreading discoloration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Absorbents, alkaline degreasers, poultices, enzyme cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Muriatic acid on oil, bleach as a degreaser</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stain sits in or on the sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild degreaser, soft brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong solvents that lift acrylic sealer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil wicks into joints and edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paver-safe degreasers, poultices</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pressure washing out joint sand</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Once you know the surface, check the age of the stain. A wet, glossy spot is fresh and mostly sitting on top. A dull, dark patch that does not feel slick is old and has migrated into the material. Fresh stains are a cleanup job; old ones on concrete are closer to a restoration project, which is why we cover them in a separate guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a> using poultices and enzymes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-hour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What to Do in the First Hour, on Any Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest factor in whether a stain disappears completely is how much oil you remove before it soaks in. On concrete, the first 30 to 60 minutes decide most of the outcome. On asphalt, speed also limits how much binder the oil can soften. Do not hose the spill down. Water spreads oil across a wider area and pushes it into cracks and toward the storm drain, which in most places is illegal to send automotive fluids into.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instead, cover the puddle with a dry absorbent. Clay-based kitty litter is the classic choice because it is cheap and widely available; choose the plain, non-clumping, unscented kind. Purpose-made granular absorbents such as Oil-Dri work the same way and are a little more aggressive at pulling oil out of pores. Sawdust, cornmeal and baking soda all work in a pinch, though they are less absorbent per pound. Spread a layer about half an inch deep and extend it an inch past the edge of the wet area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grind the absorbent in with your shoe or a brick. This step is where most people under-do it: grinding breaks the granules into finer particles, which multiplies their surface area and lets them reach into the texture of the pavement. On asphalt, use a twisting motion rather than scraping so you do not loosen surface stone. Leave the absorbent for at least a few hours, ideally overnight, then sweep it up into a bag. Oily absorbent should not go in a storm drain or compost; most municipalities accept it in household trash when the oil content is small, but check your local hazardous waste rules.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FIRST-HOUR RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Absorb first, clean second, rinse last.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every cleaner works better on a stain that has already given up its loose oil. Putting degreaser on a puddle simply emulsifies the puddle and spreads it. Pull up the free oil with absorbent, then treat what is left in the surface.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you only have dish soap and water nearby, that is still a reasonable second step once the absorbent is up. Squeeze a generous line of grease-cutting dish soap onto the spot, add a splash of water, scrub with a stiff nylon brush for a couple of minutes and let it sit for 15 minutes before rinsing with a bucket, not a hose at full blast. On a fresh spill, absorbent plus soap often leaves nothing visible on either surface. If a shadow remains after it dries, move to the surface-specific steps below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Removing Oil From an Asphalt Driveway Without Damaging It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The goal on blacktop is to lift oil while leaving the binder alone. That rules out anything labeled as a solvent degreaser, citrus solvent, mineral spirits, brake cleaner or carburetor cleaner. It also rules out the old advice to pour gasoline on a stain and burn it off, which is dangerous and destroys the surface. Stick to water-based cleaners, and keep contact times to what the label specifies. Most water-based degreasers say whether they are safe for asphalt; if the label only mentions concrete, test it on an inconspicuous corner first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The asphalt method, step by step</h3>

          <p>
            After the absorbent has done its work, wet the area lightly so the cleaner does not soak straight into dry pavement. Apply a water-based degreaser or a microbial cleaner, or a strong dish soap solution if that is what you have. Work it in with a stiff nylon brush using moderate pressure; a wire brush or a steel-bristle push broom will tear loose the fine aggregate on older asphalt. Let it dwell for 5 to 10 minutes, keep it wet, then rinse with a garden hose on a fan setting. Repeat once or twice rather than leaving the cleaner on for a long time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Enzyme and microbial cleaners such as Terminator-HSD or Pour-N-Restore are well suited to blacktop because they break down hydrocarbons without solvents. They are slower, often taking a day or more, and they need to stay damp while they work, but they do not attack the binder. Apply them, keep the spot moist according to the label, and let a few days of normal weather finish the job. Owners commonly report that a faint shadow fades over the following week as the remaining oil is digested.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washers are fine on sound asphalt at moderate settings, but they cause trouble on aging blacktop. A narrow tip or close distance will blast out fine aggregate and leave a rough, light-colored patch that looks worse than the original stain. If you use one, choose a 25-degree or 40-degree tip, keep the wand 12 inches or more from the surface, and move steadily. A <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner attachment</a> spreads the force more evenly and is gentler than a hand wand for driveway-wide cleaning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the oil has already softened the asphalt</h3>

          <p>
            Press a screwdriver into the stained area. If it sinks in more easily than in clean pavement, or the surface feels gummy, the binder has been compromised. Clean what you can using the method above, let the area dry and cure for several days, then apply an oil spot primer made for asphalt before sealing. Most driveway sealer manufacturers sell or specify one, because sealer does not bond to oily patches. If the spot is crumbling, cut it out and patch it with cold asphalt patch, then seal the whole driveway once it has cured. Our guide to the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a> covers which products include a primer step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Removing Oil From a Concrete Driveway
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete tolerates far stronger chemistry than asphalt, so you can be more aggressive. The trade-off is that concrete holds stains deeper. A driveway poured with a broom finish has more surface texture for oil to hide in, while older concrete that has weathered and lost its cream layer is more porous still. Expect a fresh spill on concrete to come out completely with one or two rounds, and expect a stain that has been there for months to need repeated treatments.
          </p>

          <p>
            After absorbing the free oil, apply a concentrated alkaline degreaser such as Oil Eater, Purple Power or Zep Driveway and Concrete Cleaner. Mix it at the strongest dilution the label allows for heavy grease, or use it straight if the instructions permit. Pre-wet the concrete slightly, apply the cleaner, and scrub hard with a stiff nylon or polypropylene brush. Concrete can take a heavier scrub than asphalt; a nylon deck brush on a pole is the most efficient tool. Let it dwell according to the label, typically 5 to 15 minutes, and do not let it dry on the surface, especially in sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse with a pressure washer if you have one. A 15-degree or 25-degree tip held 8 to 12 inches away works well on sound concrete, and hot water dramatically increases what the rinse removes because heat thins the oil. Most homeowner machines are cold water, so a second application of degreaser is often the substitute for heat. Rinse toward a lawn or gravel area rather than the street, and if your town is strict about runoff, catch the wash water with a shop vacuum.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges for Concrete Degreasing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Label dwell time for most alkaline degreasers</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rounds a weeks-old stain usually needs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wand distance for a 15 to 25 degree tip</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry time before judging the result</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Judge the result only after the concrete has dried completely. Wet concrete is darker overall, which hides a remaining stain, and then the shadow reappears the next day. If the spot is fainter but still visible after two full rounds, switching products usually helps more than repeating the same one a fourth time. A poultice draws oil up from deeper in the slab, while an enzyme cleaner works on what a degreaser leaves behind. The roundup of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> sorts the options by stain age.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing not to reach for is muriatic acid. Acid etches the cement paste and can brighten the surrounding concrete, but it does not dissolve oil, so you end up with a cleaner-looking ring around an unchanged stain. Bleach is similar: it lightens organic stains like leaves and mildew but has little effect on petroleum. Keep both out of the oil stain toolkit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pavers-sealed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pavers, Sealed Concrete and Stamped Driveways
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealed surface is easier to clean in one way and trickier in another. A good penetrating sealer slows oil down, so a fresh spill wipes up with absorbent and soap more completely than on bare concrete. But film-forming acrylic sealers, common on stamped and colored concrete, can be softened by strong solvents and some concentrated degreasers. If the cleaner leaves a hazy or white patch, it has affected the sealer, not just the stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sealed or stamped concrete, start with the mildest option and step up. Dish soap and a soft-bristle brush, then a water-based degreaser at a light dilution, then a stronger mix. Rinse with a garden hose or a pressure washer at a wide angle and moderate distance. If the oil has gone under the sealer, which happens with old acrylic films that have worn thin, the stain will not come out until the sealer is stripped in that area. At that point, plan on cleaning and resealing the section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers add the joint problem. Oil often runs off the face of a paver and soaks into the sand between units, which then acts as a reservoir that keeps re-staining the edges. Use a poultice or absorbent on both the paver and the joint. When you rinse, keep the pressure low so you do not wash out the polymeric or regular sand; if you lose some, refill the joints once everything is dry. Badly stained individual pavers can simply be lifted and flipped or replaced, which is often faster than chasing a deep stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the surface, once it is clean and fully dry, a sealer is your best defense against the next drip. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer for concrete or a dedicated <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer</a> gives you a window of time to blot oil before it sets in, which turns future drips into a five-minute job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-fluids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Transmission Fluid, Coolant, Gas and Grease
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every dark spot under a car is engine oil. The color of the drip tells you what you are dealing with and often hints at a problem with the car worth fixing before you clean anything. Red or pink spots are usually automatic transmission fluid or power steering fluid. Green, orange or pink watery puddles that feel slightly slick are coolant. Brown to black with a thick feel is engine oil, and a dark, sticky patch near the front wheels can be gear oil or grease.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transmission fluid and power steering fluid behave like light oils and respond to the same methods covered above. They tend to leave a reddish tint on concrete that a degreaser lifts well. Grease is heavier and needs scraping with a plastic putty knife before absorbent and degreaser can reach it. Gear oil can carry a strong sulfur smell, and that odor lingers until the oil is gone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coolant needs to be cleaned up quickly for a different reason: ethylene glycol is sweet tasting and toxic to pets and children. Absorb it, scrub with soap and water, rinse thoroughly, and keep animals away until it is dry. It rarely stains concrete permanently. Gasoline evaporates quickly but can leave a faint ring and does soften asphalt, so absorb it, ventilate, keep ignition sources away and let it evaporate before washing. Never use a vacuum on gasoline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brake fluid deserves a note too. It is not a petroleum oil in most cars; it is a glycol-based fluid that can strip paint and can soften some sealers. Absorb it, then clean with soap and water. If any of these leaks are recurring, park over a drip pan or a garage mat until the car is repaired, because no cleaning routine keeps up with an active leak.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. After the Stain Is Gone: Prevent the Next One
          </h2>

          <p>
            The easiest stain to remove is the one that never soaks in. A heavy-duty drip mat or a containment mat sized for the car costs roughly $20 to $60 at the time of writing and catches the slow leaks that create most driveway stains. Place it where the engine sits when you park, which is often a foot or two forward of where people guess. Check and empty it every few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing the driveway is the long-term fix. On asphalt, a fresh coat of sealer every two to three years fills surface voids and gives oil less to grab, though oil spots still need priming before you seal over them. On concrete, a penetrating sealer reduces absorption without changing the look of the slab, and many products are rated to repel oil long enough for you to blot it up. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">sealing a concrete driveway</a> walks through the prep, and the asphalt side is covered in <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a whole-driveway refresh before sealing, a general <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">driveway cleaner</a> applied with a pump sprayer and rinsed with a surface cleaner evens out the color so spot-cleaned areas do not stand out as lighter patches. That uneven look is one of the most common complaints after aggressive spot cleaning on concrete, and cleaning the whole surface is the fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep a small spill kit by the garage door: a bag of absorbent, a stiff brush and a bottle of degreaser. Most permanent driveway stains started as spills that sat over a weekend because nothing was on hand. With supplies within reach, the response time drops to minutes, and on either surface that is what decides whether a spill becomes a stain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using solvent degreasers on blacktop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Products built on petroleum solvents, citrus solvents or brake cleaner dissolve asphalt binder the same way they dissolve oil. The stain may lighten, but the surface underneath turns soft and starts to ravel under tires. On asphalt, stick to water-based degreasers, soap and enzyme cleaners, and read the label for an asphalt-safe statement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hosing a fresh spill first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water does not dissolve oil; it floats and spreads it. Hosing a fresh puddle turns a dinner-plate stain into a streak across the driveway and can push oil into the street drain. Always lay down absorbent and grind it in before any water touches the spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the result while the surface is wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet concrete is uniformly darker, which hides remaining oil. Many people stop after one round because the spot looks gone, then see it come back the next morning. Let the area dry for a full day before deciding whether it needs another treatment or a different product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing too close</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A zero-degree or 15-degree tip held a few inches from the surface can etch concrete and strip aggregate from asphalt, leaving a lighter, rougher patch that is more visible than the stain. Use a wider tip, keep a foot of distance on asphalt, and keep the wand moving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over an oily patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driveway sealer and concrete sealer both struggle to bond to oil. Sealing over a stain often leads to peeling or a dark spot bleeding through within a season. Clean the area, let it dry, and apply an oil spot primer on asphalt before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does kitty litter really work on driveway oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for fresh spills. Plain clay litter absorbs free oil well, especially if you grind it into the surface with your shoe so the granules break down and reach into the texture. It works on both asphalt and concrete. It does little for old, set-in stains, because by then the oil has moved below the surface where the litter cannot reach it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dish soap safe on an asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grease-cutting dish soap is one of the safest cleaners for blacktop because it is water-based and does not attack the asphalt binder. Use a generous amount, scrub with a nylon brush, let it sit for about 15 minutes and rinse well. It handles fresh and light stains; heavier ones usually need a water-based degreaser or an enzyme cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the same degreaser on asphalt and concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the label says it is safe for asphalt. Many alkaline, water-based degreasers are, but solvent-based and citrus solvent products are not. When in doubt, test a small spot near the edge of the driveway and check after it dries for softening, tackiness or loose stone before treating the main stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to get oil off a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fresh spill on either surface usually takes an evening: absorbent overnight, then 30 minutes of cleaning the next day. A stain that has sat for weeks on concrete may need two or three rounds over several days. Enzyme cleaners on asphalt can take a week to finish working, with little effort from you in the meantime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will oil stains damage my asphalt permanently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can. Oil softens the asphalt binder, and a stain left for weeks may leave a spot that crumbles or stays tacky. Small, cleaned-up spots usually recover once the surface is primed and sealed. Larger soft areas should be cut out and patched with cold patch asphalt before you seal the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to rinse oil cleaner into the street?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Storm drains in most areas flow directly to streams and lakes without treatment, and many local codes prohibit washing automotive fluids or degreaser residue into them. Rinse onto gravel or lawn where allowed, or collect wash water with a wet vacuum and dispose of it through your local household hazardous waste program.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I clean the whole driveway after spot cleaning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On concrete, often yes. Spot degreasing can leave a noticeably lighter patch because it removes years of general grime along with the oil. A whole-surface wash with a driveway cleaner and a surface cleaner attachment evens the color. On asphalt, sealing the driveway after spot cleaning does the same job and protects the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

