        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Treat the two surfaces differently and most driveway oil stains come out. On asphalt, absorb fast and stay with water-based cleaners and enzymes, because the pavement itself is made of the same family of chemicals as the stain. On concrete, absorb fast and then use a strong alkaline degreaser and a firm scrub, repeating until the dry surface looks clean.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If a concrete stain survives two full rounds, it has moved deeper than a degreaser can reach, and a poultice or enzyme treatment is the next step. Once the surface is clean and dry, seal it, and keep a spill kit by the garage so the next leak never gets a chance to set.
          </p>
        </div>

